A DEFINITION WHOSE LABEL WRAPS ACROSS LINES IS DROPPED, AND ITS USE IS
RESOLVED INLINE.

WHAT md DOES TODAY. The whole output is one line:

    Uses [foo bar](/url).

The definition is gone and the reference spelling with it. This is not
a fold problem: the same input WITH the blank line, as this case is
written, fails the same way, so it predates the folded-definition work
and was found during it (defect 28).

WHAT IT SHOULD DO. Keep both, byte for byte, like every single-line
definition: the wrapped `[foo\nbar]: /url` block and the `[foo bar]`
reference.

CAUSE. Both recovery grammars read a definition's label from ONE line.
`definitionLabel(inLine:)` scans each line of an uncovered span for
`[label]:`, so `[foo` on the first line yields no label, the span fails
`isDefinitionShaped`, and the definition is never written back. With no
recovered label, `restoreReferenceStyleLinks` has nothing to match and
the `[foo bar]` link keeps cmark's pasted-URL form. The folded-paragraph
scanner (`leadingDefinitionLineCount`) is single-line-label in the same
way.

THE FIX NEEDS BOTH GRAMMARS EXTENDED TOGETHER. A label may contain up
to one line ending (labels normalize internal whitespace, which is how
`[foo\nbar]` matches `[foo bar]`). Extending only the span grammar
would recover the definition while the link still resolves inline,
duplicating the URL — the same trap defect 27 documents. Keep the
strict-or-equal-to-cmark rule from the grammar audit: a multi-line
label parser must not accept anything cmark rejects, such as a label
with a blank line inside it or one over cmark's length cap.
